value.fix:
Stop sending the whole `user` object back. `res.json({ user })` serialises every field on the model, which can include the password hash, reset or verification tokens, internal flags, timestamps and other Personal Data Elements the requester has no right to see. Replace it with an explicit allow-list of response fields kept in one shared serializer, for example:

```js
// serializers/user.js
const PUBLIC_USER_FIELDS = ['id', 'name', 'email', 'role'];
function toUserResponse(user, { viewer } = {}) {
  const src = typeof user.toObject === 'function' ? user.toObject() : user;
  const out = {};
  for (const k of PUBLIC_USER_FIELDS) if (src[k] !== undefined) out[k] = src[k];
  if (src._id && !out.id) out.id = String(src._id);
  // only add owner-only fields when the requester is the data principal or an authorised admin
  if (viewer && (String(viewer.id) === String(out.id) || viewer.role === 'admin')) {
    out.phone = src.phone;
  }
  return out;
}
module.exports = { toUserResponse };
```

Then in the handler: `res.json({ user: toUserResponse(user, { viewer: req.user }) })`.

Also limit the fields at the query itself (for example Mongoose `.select('id name email role')`, Prisma `select: {...}`, or Sequelize `attributes: [...]`) so sensitive columns are never loaded. Leave nested relations out unless they also go through their own allow-list serializer. Use `toUserResponse` on every route that returns a user record (login, register, /me, profile, admin lists and so on) so the field limits are the same everywhere, as Rule 6(1)(b) requires. Finally, add a test that asserts the response body has no `password`, `passwordHash`, `resetToken`, `__v` or similar keys. This change has not been applied or tested in the repo yet.
